=== WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES ===

WORKS. A chain's depth is const-available without a forbidden feature. The
refused spelling is `Fx<{D + 1}>`, arithmetic in a type argument, which needs
generic_const_exprs. The accepted one carries depth as a TYPE and the number
as an associated const, so the increment is arithmetic in a value position and
ordinary const evaluation handles it. That is the workspace's standing reflex
landing on a case nobody had pointed it at: a refused bound wants a trait.

And the const predicate reaches one lowered path, which is what I15 asks for.
Zero conditional branches in all three functions. `chain_switch_at_2` shows the
switch WITHIN one function: its first step emits `mul` with no rounding
constant and its last three emit `madd` against #128, which is truncate at
depth 1 and round at depths 2, 3 and 4, exactly what the type says, with
nothing left to decide at run time.

=== AND ONE THING THAT CUTS AGAINST p3, WHICH IS WHY IT IS HERE ===

This is an ad-hoc quick spike with no substance for any how-much question. It
is not a bench, it took no timing, and nothing below prices anything.

With that said: the three emitted sequences are 9, 10 and 10 instructions.
Rounding to nearest costs one hoisted `mov` for the whole chain, because this
target fuses the add-half into `madd`. p3's second cost coordinate is a count
of round-to-nearest steps, and on this target that count does not correspond
to a per-step cost at all.

So p3's arm family is real as an accuracy structure and its TRADE is a fact
about the target, not about the arithmetic. On a target with a fused
multiply-add the interior of that Pareto front may collapse to its round-
everywhere end. That is unpriced, it needs the harness rather than this, and
it is the first thing I would attack next.

It is also `25` section 7's own clause arriving with evidence: an assignment on
an axis is a function of the build condition, and here is an axis whose very
EXISTENCE as a trade is a function of the build condition.
